msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/build_wasm_emscripten.md:292
msgid "Viewing unit test output"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/build_wasm_emscripten.md:294
msgid ""
"You may run the tests with the verbose flag to view all messages sent to "
"`cout` and `cerr`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/build_wasm_emscripten.md:296
msgid "$ ctest -R FooUnitTest -VV\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/build_wasm_emscripten.md:300
msgid "Running unit tests interactively"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/build_wasm_emscripten.md:302
msgid ""
"In order to run the unit test interactively, you will need to determine the "
"exact program and arguments that `ctest` uses to run your unit test. You can "
"see the test command for a test named `FooUnitTest` by running `ctest -R "
"FooUnitTest -N -V`. Now, reconstruct the test command line and add a `-I` "
"argument at the end. This will keep the browser open because the unit test "
"is in interactive mode. If your test uses data or a valid baseline, you will "
"require the HTTP server. When run using `ctest`, the HTTP server is "
"automatically started before your test runs and shut down after the test "
"completes. On the other hand, if you run the test manually by copying the "
"test command into the console, you will need to run `ctest -R vtkWasm-"
"HTTPServerSetup` by hand before executing the test. Do not forget to run "
"`ctest -R vtkWasm-HTTPServerCleanup` when you are finished. The wasm test "
"suite is explained in detail at [WebAssemblyTestSuiteArchitecture](../"
"design_documents/WebAssemblyTestSuiteArchitecture.md)."
msgstr ""

